According to the documented rule for conformance.kernel.recovery, 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' [65ae18af7a738b26]. In the input fence[ A B ] fence[ C ], there are two code fences. The first holds objects A and B, the second holds object C. Since fences hold objects, the last object inside a code fence is scored. C is in the second (last) fence, so C is the scored object. In the rewritten text fence[ A B ] fence[ ], object C is removed from the second fence, leaving it empty. The first fence still holds objects A and B. Since B is now the last object inside any code fence, the scored object changes from C to B. Thus the recovered object moves from {"c": 3} to {"b": 2}.